\ Compiler design constraints
\ - Need not be bootstrapping - that is, the compiler is a HOST system that
\   generates code for a target that does not have its own Forth compiler.
\ - Must respect present vs. stripped names.
\ - Must support separating read-only and mutable data, including *inlining*
\   CONSTANTs.
\ - Adds a new pair of words for use with CREATEd data: r/o-data and r/w-data;
\   that indicates whether , or c, compiled data is ROM-friendly or not.

\ Compilation workflow =======================================================
\ In order to compile a regular word, say +, we need to hook into its
\ metacompilation. That happens by *executing* the mirror words, which examine
\ HOST STATE and executes either mirror>compile or mirror>interp accordingly.
\ The default mirror>compile is mirror-compile, which reads mirror>txt and
\ does a tcompile,.

\ For the compiler, we want mirror>compile to be the compiling function itself.
\ So the compiler will create mirror words with (TCREATE) and then override
\ their mirror>compile actions with custom compiling logic.

\ IMMEDIATE words ============================================================
\ These are mirror words whose metacompilation action is overwritten with its
\ HOST ACTS: ... ; block. For a non-interactive target, the target-side code of
\ an IMMEDIATE word can be thrown away, since it will never be called.
\ It can also just be ignored as dead code, if you're not concerned about space.

\ XTs in a compiled app ======================================================
\ One other concern is that a compiled word like + does not necessarily have
\ a target xt. That's not usually very important for a directly compiled app,
\ but there might be a use for eg. dynamic dispatch.
\ It can be worked around by defining a high-level word and using its xt.


\ Calling convention and register allocation =================================
\ As a first pass, I'll adopt the following approach for the DCPU:
\ - Same as original threaded code: TOS in memory, use the PUSH/POP operands.
\ - DUP, SWAP, etc. are all reified directly onto the stack
\ - This is slow and bulky, but it's simple. To be refined later.
\ - No attempt to do anything fancy at control points, just push everything
\   like a call site.

\ JSR on DCPU will leave the return address in TOS, so we have to pop it into
\ RSP and adjust RSP.
\ TODO: That could be cached in I or J, since those are free in this scheme?
\ Alternatively, could use I or J as RSP empty-descending, and STD/STI?
\ Then RSP pushes and pops are 1 word, 2 cycles.

\ Inlined native code ========================================================
\ This word defines a new mirror with the given name, and makes the following
\ (host) colon definition its mirror>compile.
: :I ( "name" [code...] -- )
  (TCREATE) :noname ;

: ;I ( -- )
  postpone ;   ( host-xt )
  'mirror @ mirror>compile ! ; IMMEDIATE

\ Arithmetic =================================================================
:I +        pop peek add, ;I
:I -        pop peek sub, ;I \ This is correct NOS - TOS order!
:I *        pop peek mli, ;I
:I /        pop peek dvi, ;I
:I MOD      pop peek mdi, ;I
:I U/       pop peek div, ;I
:I UMOD     pop peek mod, ;I
:I AND      pop peek and, ;I
:I OR       pop peek bor, ;I
:I XOR      pop peek xor, ;I
:I LSHIFT   pop peek shl, ;I
:I RSHIFT   pop peek shr, ;I
:I ARSHIFT  pop peek asr, ;I
:I 2*       1 # peek shl, ;I
:I 2/       1 # peek asr, ;I

:I INVERT   $ffff # peek xor, ;I
:I NEGATE
  0 # ra set,
  pop ra sub,
  ra push set,
;I


\ Comparisons ================================================================
\ Compiles a Forth comparison, with a flexible comparator op.
: COMPARISON, ( if-xt -- )
  >R
  0 # ra set,
  pop pop   R> execute
    -1 # ra set,
  ra push set, ;

:I =   ['] ife, comparison, ;I
:I <   ['] ifu, comparison, ;I
:I U<  ['] ifl, comparison, ;I


\ Stack ops ==================================================================
:I DUP     peek   push set, ;I
:I DROP    1 #    sp   add, ;I
:I OVER    1 picc push set, ;I

:I SWAP
  pop  ra   set,
  pop  rb   set,
  ra   push set,
  rb   push set,
;I

:I ?DUP
  0 # peek ifn,
    peek push set,
;I

:I NIP ( y x -- x )
  pop ra   set,
  ra  peek set,
;I

:I SP@   sp  push set,   ;I
:I SP!   pop sp   set,   ;I
:I RP@   rz  push set,   ;I
:I RP!   pop rz   set,   ;I

:I >R    pop  pushrsp,   ;I
:I R>    push poprsp,    ;I
:I R@    [z]  push set,  ;I


\ Memory access ==============================================================
: fetch,
  pop ra   set,
  [a] push set, ;
: store,
  pop ra   set,
  pop [a]  set, ;

:I @   fetch, ;I
:I C@  fetch, ;I
:I !   store, ;I
:I C!  store, ;I

\ These store as hi, lo in memory, but push as ( lo hi ) double-words.
:I 2@
  pop    ra   set,
  1 [a+] push set,
  [a]    push set,
;I

:I 2!
  pop    ra   set,
  pop    [a]  set,
  pop  1 [a+] set,
;I

:I +!
  pop ra  set,
  pop [a] add,
;I


\ Miscellany =================================================================

\ EXECUTE on a native code machine is a nonprimitive call, a JSR.
:I EXECUTE   pop jsr,   ;I

\ Using the builtin DCPU-16 LOG and BRK instructions.
:I LOG   ( x -- )   pop log,   ;I
:I BREAK ( x -- )   pop brk,   ;I


\ These are fake inlines - they throw, since executing these at runtime makes
\ no sense!
:I ,   71 throw ;I
:I C,  72 throw ;I


\ CODE words =================================================================
\ CODE words are written in assembly, but are NOT inlined!
\ These are called using JSR like nonprimitives.

\ NOTE: It's vital to remember that the DCPU pushes the return address as TOS!
\ That is visible to CODE words, since they might do different things with it.

\ A CODE word gets compiled just like a nonprimitive: a JSR to its absolute
\ address should be compiled. Therefore to create a CODE word, just (TCREATE)
\ to get a mirror. The default mirror>compile calls TCOMPILE, with the t-xt,
\ which is exactly what we want!

: CODE-BARE     ( "name" -- )
  (TCREATE)
  ." CODE " 'mirror @   dup mirror>mirror-nt @ id. ." at " mirror>txt @ hex. cr ;
: END-CODE-BARE ( -- )        ; \ Does nothing by default.

\ By default, CODE words use I as the scratch register for the return address.
\ That's handy because the threaded version uses I for IP, so a few words that
\ use STI and STD have logic to save I.

\ Use CODE-BARE and END-CODE-BARE if you don't want to put the return address
\ in I, and then return to it.
: save-ret,   pop ri set, ;
: return,     ri  pc set, ;

: CODE ( "name" -- ) CODE-BARE save-ret, ;
: END-CODE ( -- )    return, END-CODE-BARE ;


TVARIABLE var_up



\ Variables and core states ==================================================
\ TODO: I've omitted a bunch of these, keeping only the ones that are needed
\ for a compiled app (ie. no Forth interpreter).
\ TODO: Things like BASE and number output are useful even in eg. a game
\ without a terminal; that should become a more-specific config flag.

TVARIABLE var_handler

\ NOTE: Skipping a pile of words like , and STATE here that are only needed
\ for a target-side Forth.
:I HANDLER    var_handler # push set,   ;I
:I UP         var_up      # push set,   ;I


\ Bigger stack manipulations =================================================
:I 2DUP
  1 picc push set,
  1 picc push set,
;I

:I 2OVER
  3 picc push set,
  3 picc push set,
;I

:I 2DROP   2 # sp add,   ;I


\ More arithmetic ============================================================
:I 1+      1 # peek add,   ;I
:I CELL+   1 # peek add,   ;I
:I CHAR+   1 # peek add,   ;I
:I 1-      1 # peek sub,   ;I
:I CELL    1 # push set,   ;I

\ No-ops on DCPU.
:I CELLS   ;I
:I CHARS   ;I


\ Double-cell arithmetic =====================================================
\ D>S is DROP
:I D>S   1 # sp add,  ;I


\ Branches ===================================================================
\ Branches are abstracted, because they differ signficantly on STC systems,
\ and even on others they might prefer relative or absolute branches.
\ ,BRANCH ,DEST and !DEST handle that abstraction between the kernel and the
\ shared high-level Forth control structures.

\ For compiled code, these are inlined directly and no implementation is needed
\ here.


\ EXIT uses the exit, compiler defined by the model.
:I EXIT ( --   R: addr -- ) exit, ;I


\ DO LOOP helpers ============================================================
:I UNLOOP ( --    R: limit index -- )
  2 # rz add,
;I

:I I ( -- index    R: index -- index )
  [z] push set,
;I

:I J ( -- index1   R: index1 limit2 index2 -- index1 limit2 index2 )
  2 [z+] push set,
;I

\ TODO: I skipped the parsing helpers, since they're not needed here.
\ Those could go in the shared code and be conditionally included.

REQUIRE ../kernel/code_words.ft


\ Interrupts =================================================================
\ interrupt_handler is what actually gets IAS'd.
\ It's a machine code routine that sets up a Forth thread with the high-level
\ interrupt routine and its own tail routine.
\ The interrupt message is in A and the old A was pushed onto the stack. Since
\ we might be mid-routine elsewhere, we need to preserve that A!
\ The stack effect of the user handler is ( msg -- )
:I IRQ+ ( -- )   0 # iaq,   ;I \ Interrupts on  = queueing disabled
:I IRQ- ( -- )   1 # iaq,   ;I \ Interrupts off = queueing enabled


TVARIABLE interrupt-xt

code (>INTERRUPT) ( xt -- )
  pop   interrupt-xt [#]  set,  \ Store the target xt for interrupts.
end-code

label interrupt_handler
  \ First a special case - if the interrupt word is 0, just bail since no one is
  \ listening.
  0 #   interrupt-xt [#] ife,
    0 # rfi,

  \ Gotta save ~all the registers, since Forth words might clobber nearly any
  \ of them. Don't need to save Z or A (A is already saved; Z is handled safely)
  rb push set,   rc push set,   rx push set,
  ry push set,   ri push set,   rj push set,

  interrupt-xt [#] jsr,   \ Jump to the user handler

  \ Restoring JIYXCB - A is already saved, and we're using Z.
  pop rj set,   pop ri set,   pop ry set,
  pop rx set,   pop rc set,   pop rb set,
  \ Everything is reset; return from the interrupt.
  0 # rfi,


REQUIRE ../kernel/init.ft
